{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/gs at exact clean pushed head 13f2e5650ce2b2dfdce5aea835b05218a2d4f2eb, branch request/ancestry-cost-race-repair. Fulfils request f9e833ad under promise 8003f3fa. Main went red on my merge, so this closes a defect I introduced into the board's state.\n\nREAD THE EPISTEMIC STATUS FIRST, BECAUSE IT IS NOT THE USUAL ONE. I did not reproduce the failure. This head removes the one mechanism consistent with the evidence that I can find and remove, and that removal is correct on its own terms; it is not a diagnosis confirmed by a red-to-green transition. A reviewer should weigh it as hardening with a strong independent justification, not as a proven repair, and I would rather say that than let a green suite imply more.\n\nWHAT FAILED. Run 32569672912: TempDir RemoveAll cleanup: unlinkat .../repo/.git: directory not empty, in TestAncestryCostsTheSameWhateverTheNumberOfHeads under -race. Something recreated an entry inside .git after RemoveAll had emptied it. The test body itself passed; only cleanup failed.\n\nWHAT I RULED OUT, BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY READING. Forty runs on this host pass: two full cmd/gs packages under -race, thirty focused iterations, eight earlier. I put a logging shim on PATH for the whole fixture and captured every Git invocation: sixty-four commits and one init, with no gc, maintenance, repack or commit-graph, and no process left alive holding the repository. Sixty-five loose objects is far under gc.auto's 6700, and gc.auto and gc.autoDetach are unset here. Every call site waits -- git() and testGit use CombinedOutput, cat-file uses Output -- so nothing in our code detaches. My first hypothesis was background auto-gc and the object count falsified it before I wrote any code.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY WRONG, INDEPENDENT OF THIS RUN. The fixture inherits whatever Git configuration the machine carries. A test whose entire claim is an exact process count cannot afford that: a runner-level gc.auto or maintenance.auto starts work the fixture never asked for, detached work outlives the test body, and it writes into .git precisely while TempDir is removing it. internal/docset already pins GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL and GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM for its Git fixtures; cmd/gs pins nothing. That inconsistency is a defect whether or not it explains run 32569672912.\n\nGIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 is the narrower half and the closer match to the symptom: it stops read-only commands taking optional locks, so answering a question cannot write into .git as a side effect. An entry reappearing in .git after RemoveAll emptied it is exactly that shape.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO, per your conditions. No sleep -- a sleep turns a deterministic claim into a slower coin flip. No tolerated cleanup failure -- TempDir failing to remove a directory is real signal that something still holds it, and silencing it would hide the next occurrence. No rm anywhere. And the claim is intact: mutating classifyHeads to run one extra Git process per head still fails with \"ancestry ran 4 git processes for 2 heads and 66 for 64, want exactly 2 for each\", so the fixture still counts what it says it counts.\n\nWHY THE FAILURE REACHED MAIN AT ALL, WHICH IS THE PART I OWN. My gates for the bounded-status head ran go test -count=1 ./... and not -race. The failure is race-only; the ordinary suite passed in CI too. I had been saying \"full suite green\" from a run that could not have caught it. This head is gated with -race and that is now part of what I mean by gated, asked for or not.\n\nGATES at the exact head, all run: gofmt clean; go vet ./... clean; go test -race -count=1 ./cmd/gs 151.028s; go test -race -count=1 ./... with no failing package; go test -count=1 ./... with no failing package; git diff --check clean. Worktree clean, based on current main e9ec6070, pushed.","body":{"branch":"request/ancestry-cost-race-repair","commit":"13f2e5650ce2b2dfdce5aea835b05218a2d4f2eb","path":"cmd/gs","tests":"gofmt; go vet ./...; go test -race -count=1 ./cmd/gs; go test -race -count=1 ./...; go test -count=1 ./...; git diff --check; mutation confirming the exact process-count claim still fails when violated"}}